<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Space exploration</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Space exploration is the physical investigation of outer space by uncrewed robotic space probes and through human spaceflight, made possible by large rockets in the mid-twentieth century. Its early era was driven by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/space-race">Space Race</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>: the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sputnik-1">Sputnik 1</a> became the first human-made object to orbit Earth on 4 October 1957, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a> made the first human spaceflight aboard Vostok 1 in 1961, and the American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> 11 mission made the first Moon landing on 20 July 1969. From the 1970s the focus moved to reusable hardware such as the Space Shuttle and to cooperation, above all the International Space Station, built between 1998 and 2011.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/space-exploration.jpg" alt="Space exploration" width="330" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Project Apollo Archive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AS11-40-5964_(21037459754).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
